<Article
  title="How it broke"
  description="How a compile-time guard tested macro presence rather than value, allowing the software fallback to serve the affected seed-generation path."
  standfirst="A compile-time guard tested macro presence rather than value, allowing the software fallback to serve the affected seed-generation path from 2021 to 2026. This section explains how that happened; whether it includes you is answered under Am I affected?"
  depth="deep"
  kicker="How it broke"
  next={{ href: '/how-it-broke/blast-radius/', label: 'What else a compromised seed touches', note: 'every randomness call site in the firmware, from BIP85 children and Secure Notes to the 2FA nonces and seed-XOR shares' }}
>
  <Answer>
    <p>
      COLDCARD firmware asks for random bytes through a call that was supposed
      to reach the STM32's hardware noise circuit. A compile-time guard tested
      whether a configuration macro existed rather than what it was set to, so
      on the affected build that call resolved instead to MicroPython's software
      fallback: a small pseudo-random generator seeded from the chip's
      factory-programmed unique ID and its timers. From March 2021 until the
      2026 hotfix, seeds generated on the device came from that fallback, XORed
      with a second software generator whose starting state is a published
      constant. Mk4, Mk5 and Q additionally mix in a secure-element value on the
      normal boot path, but only 32 bits of it, and only into one of the two
      generators.<Claim
        basis="verified"
        scope="The symbol-resolution path, generator substitution and 32-bit reseed behaviour documented on this page"
        source="COLDCARD firmware, switck/libngu and COLDCARD MicroPython source at the cited revisions"
        href="/record/firmware/"
        compact
      /> Whether your own wallet is affected is answered under
      <a href="/affected/">Am I affected?</a>
    </p>
  </Answer>

  <div class="ported" set:html={intro} />

